1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,138
Welcome to lesson three: YAML.

2
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:15,680
Every Kubernetes object is written in YAML, and so are Helm charts and CI pipelines. Most broken manifests are not Kubernetes mistakes. They're YAML mistakes. Let's make sure you can read and write it correctly.

3
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:19,360
YAML has only three building blocks.

4
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:23,822
Scalars are single values: strings, numbers, booleans and null.

5
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:30,588
Maps are key-value pairs, like a Map in Java or an object in JSON. A colon must be followed by a space.

6
00:00:30,688 --> 00:00:34,474
And lists are sequences, where each item starts with a dash.

7
00:00:35,824 --> 00:00:40,858
Kubernetes combines these all the time. The most common pattern is a list of maps.

8
00:00:40,958 --> 00:00:48,072
Here, containers is a list. Each dash starts a new container. The lines aligned under name belong to the same container.

9
00:00:48,172 --> 00:00:54,438
By the way, YAML is a superset of JSON, so kubectl also accepts JSON files.

10
00:00:55,788 --> 00:00:59,974
Indentation is how YAML shows structure, so the rules are strict.

11
00:01:00,074 --> 00:01:04,084
Use spaces only, never tabs. A tab causes an error.

12
00:01:04,184 --> 00:01:10,658
Children are indented more than their parent, usually by two spaces, and siblings must line up exactly.

13
00:01:12,008 --> 00:01:17,554
Here's the most dangerous kind of mistake: YAML that is valid, but means something different.

14
00:01:17,654 --> 00:01:24,784
The resources block is one level too far to the left. It still parses, but now it belongs to the Pod spec instead of the container.

15
00:01:24,884 --> 00:01:33,934
Depending on the tool, it's rejected or silently ignored, and your memory limit never applies. For every field, ask: which parent does this belong to?

16
00:01:35,284 --> 00:01:39,982
Most strings don't need quotes, but some values change type unless you quote them.

17
00:01:40,082 --> 00:01:48,204
Version one point ten becomes the number one point one. Yes and no can become booleans. A zip code with a leading zero can become an octal number.

18
00:01:48,304 --> 00:01:54,570
And values starting with special characters, like the at sign, cause errors. When in doubt, quote it.

19
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:59,786
In Kubernetes, one quoting rule matters more than any other.

20
00:01:59,886 --> 00:02:04,552
Environment variable values, labels and annotations must be strings.

21
00:02:04,652 --> 00:02:10,454
So a port number or true must be in quotes. AI-generated manifests get this wrong very often.

22
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:15,334
For multi-line text, YAML has two styles.

23
00:02:15,434 --> 00:02:20,644
The pipe keeps every newline exactly. Use it for scripts and config files.

24
00:02:20,744 --> 00:02:25,106
The greater-than sign folds the lines into one, joined with spaces.

25
00:02:26,456 --> 00:02:28,018
A few more features.

26
00:02:28,118 --> 00:02:32,976
Three dashes separate several objects in one file, and a hash starts a comment.

27
00:02:33,076 --> 00:02:38,478
Anchors and aliases let you reuse a block, though Helm templates are usually preferred.

28
00:02:38,578 --> 00:02:44,572
Watch out for duplicate keys. Most parsers silently keep the last one, with no warning.

29
00:02:45,922 --> 00:02:47,500
Validate in layers.

30
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,538
First check that it's valid YAML at all. Then use yamllint for style and common mistakes.

31
00:02:52,638 --> 00:03:02,296
Finally check it against Kubernetes itself with kubectl apply dash dash dry run, and use kubectl explain to see which fields exist and where they belong.

32
00:03:03,646 --> 00:03:07,736
In the lab, you'll create a file with three bugs and find them with yamllint.

33
00:03:07,836 --> 00:03:15,446
RETRIES has a number where a string is needed. The VERSION item is indented by the wrong amount. And one point ten would become one point one.

34
00:03:15,546 --> 00:03:20,948
A great trick: convert the YAML to JSON. The braces and brackets make the structure obvious.

35
00:03:22,298 --> 00:03:23,540
Let's recap.

36
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:29,746
YAML is built from scalars, maps and lists, and Kubernetes mostly uses lists of maps.

37
00:03:29,846 --> 00:03:35,296
Use spaces only, align siblings, and check which parent each field belongs to.

38
00:03:35,396 --> 00:03:39,742
Quote environment values, labels, annotations and version numbers.

39
00:03:39,842 --> 00:03:45,804
And remember: valid YAML isn't necessarily valid Kubernetes, so always validate.

40
00:03:45,904 --> 00:03:49,994
In the next lesson, you'll create your first Kubernetes cluster.
